          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry you've been struggling with sleep for this long. The risk check found no emergency pattern in what you described. That does not rule out a problem, so if things get worse or you develop new symptoms, contact a clinician.\n\nBased on the retrieved guidance:\n\n- **Insomnia** is difficulty falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early, despite having enough time to sleep, along with daytime effects such as tiredness or poor concentration. Short bouts often pass on their own. Trouble that lasts at least three nights a week for three months or more is classed as chronic insomnia. (Source: \"Insomnia,\" general clinical reference; \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment,\" sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance.)\n- The same guidance says a clinician usually looks for other causes first. These include sleep apnea, restless legs, depression, anxiety, pain, caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, shift work, and effects of medicines. It also suggests keeping a sleep diary for one to two weeks. (Source: the sleep medicine guidance above.)\n- For chronic insomnia, the guidance describes cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia as the first-line treatment. (Source: the same guideline.)\n\nI could not find anything in the retrieved material about how severe your sleep problem is, what might be causing it in your case, or which treatments would suit you. Please talk with a doctor or other clinician. Bring a note of when you have trouble sleeping and how it affects your days. Ask whether any other causes should be checked and whether therapy would be a good option.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
