          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is medication the usual answer?",
          "answer": "For chronic insomnia, the retrieved guidance does not treat medication as the usual first choice. Sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance names cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) as first-line, ahead of medication. It says the benefits of CBT-I last after treatment ends, while medication effects generally do not, and CBT-I avoids the harms linked to long-term sleep medicines. (Source: \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment.\") The guidance describes CBT-I as a course of roughly four to eight sessions. It includes methods such as keeping the bed for sleep only and adjusting time in bed.\n\nThe retrieved material does not say when medication might be added, which medicines are used, or how they compare with therapy for your situation. Whether either option suits you is a question for a clinician who knows your full history. Some guidance, such as for anxiety, lists medication as a first-line option alongside therapy. Those passages cover other conditions, so I have not applied them to insomnia.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
